fa788-f255-5481-b240-31ebb5aaebdc" 13194139534186 true] [17592186281211 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980753/assets/88b379da88c9c00be0aa64557dfdccef6bd503a894d56f5a89a13b081db2ab14.png" 13194139534186 true] [17592186281211 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534186 true] [17592186281211 1111 #uuid "76d2e1fd-b3e8-550f-94bd-fc22732150bd" 13194139534186 true] [17592186281212 1017 #edb/ref 17592186281213 13194139534186 true] [17592186281212 1089 "q-104124" 13194139534186 true] [17592186281212 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534186 true] [17592186281212 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980753/assets/bdcfa35fb04028cbff32992b41214c878443d3815b676a0cf046f5a99bd771bd.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $f(x)$ is **not** defined at $x=-2$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=f(-2)$\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-2$" 13194139534186 true] [17592186281212 1113 #uuid "af1da89e-6dd1-5d6d-99dd-762b173b6a23" 13194139534186 true] [17592186281212 1145 "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=-2$ and $f(-2)=5.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, from the graph we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{-}}f(x) & =5, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,-{2}^{+}}f(x) & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}f(x)=5=f(-2).$\n\n- Statement III is true. The graph of $f(x)$ can be drawn without taking our pencil off of the paper in the neighborhood of $x=-2.$" 13194139534186 true] [17592186281213 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281214 13194139534186 true] [17592186281213 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281215 13194139534186 true] [17592186281213 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281216 13194139534186 true] [17592186281213 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281217 13194139534186 true] [17592186281213 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281218 13194139534186 true] [17592186281213 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534186 true] [17592186281213 1041 "selection" 13194139534186 true] [17592186281213 1104 #edb/ref 17592186281214 13194139534186 true] [17592186281213 1110 #uuid "32321e97-b95f-52de-bf4b-99684620297d" 13194139534186 true] [17592186281214 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534186 true] [17592186281214 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534186 true] [17592186281214 1111 #uuid "0ccba1ce-df30-5537-9903-3539c59244a1" 13194139534186 true] [17592186281215 1034 "I only" 13194139534186 true] [17592186281215 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534186 true] [17592186281215 1111 #uuid "4f4c715d-922e-5f15-a7c5-7d8861065287" 13194139534186 true] [17592186281216 1034 "III only" 13194139534186 true] [17592186281216 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534186 true] [17592186281216 1111 #uuid "d9318cab-6240-5247-92be-6dbae81f6d7c" 13194139534186 true] [17592186281217 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534186 true] [17592186281217 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534186 true] [17592186281217 1111 #uuid "060f2bab-5815-509c-87b6-cca9bc209c9a" 13194139534186 true] [17592186281218 1034 "II only" 13194139534186 true] [17592186281218 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534186 true] [17592186281218 1111 #uuid "d7ada344-5734-567e-8b91-6b033af9fcfa" 13194139534186 true] [17592186281219 1017 #edb/ref 17592186281220 13194139534186 true] [17592186281219 1089 "q-48040" 13194139534186 true] [17592186281219 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534186 true] [17592186281219 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980753/assets/59c02273515e87fcebb8eb1785dc2cf0c6490feda4fc9644df43b8849921eec5.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $f(x)$ is defined at $x=-1$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)$ exists\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1$" 13194139534186 true] [17592186281219 1113 #uuid "9a5d50fd-2105-50c1-b271-583fe4966ec5" 13194139534186 true] [17592186281219 1145 "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function $f(x)$ is defined at $x=-1$ and we have $f(-1)=1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x) & =2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{+}}f(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, both left-hand side and right-hand side limits exist but they are not the same. Therefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)$ does not exist.\n\n- Statement III is false. The function contains a break exactly at $x=-1$ and it's impossible to draw its graph without taking our pencil off of the paper." 13194139534186 true] [17592186281220 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281221 13194139534186 true] [17592186281220 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281222 13194139534186 true] [17592186281220 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281223 13194139534186 true] [17592186281220 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281224 13194139534186 true] [17592186281220 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281225 13194139534186 true] [17592186281220 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534186 true] [17592186281220 1041 "selection" 13194139534186 true] [17592186281220 1104 #edb/ref 17592186281222 13194139534186 true] [17592186281220 1110 #uuid "dfefd75d-07e6-504b-bb74-0ac875813837" 13194139534186 true] [17592186281221 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534186 true] [17592186281221 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534186 true] [17592186281221 1111 #uuid "4c5e13a8-bedb-550c-b1e6-d42a04636ba7" 13194139534186 true] [17592186281222 1034 "I only" 13194139534186 true] [17592186281222 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534186 true] [17592186281222 1111 #uuid "d0211631-c43d-5c39-9af5-32714e3b79fe" 13194139534186 true] [17592186281223 1034 "III only" 13194139534186 true] [17592186281223 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534186 true] [17592186281223 1111 #uuid "75b34151-a605-554a-90dc-34445553f367" 13194139534186 true] [17592186281224 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534186 true] [17592186281224 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534186 true] [17592186281224 1111 #uuid "43ec9045-664a-5087-908c-b8ecfed1728a" 